### 📊 **ML & DL Algorithms: Box Type, Explanation & Best-Fit Statistical Test (with Justifications)**

| Algorithm                     | Box Type | Explanation                                                                                   | Best Statistical Test(s)         | ✅ Why this test? / ❌ Why not others? |
|------------------------------|----------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| **Linear Regression**         | White    | Coefficients are interpretable; simple linear relationships                                  | ✅ **t-test**, **p-value**, **ANOVA** | ✅ Tests significance of coefficients. ❌ Chi-square is for categorical, not continuous outcomes. |
| **Logistic Regression**       | White    | Coefficients indicate log-odds impact; interpretable                                          | ✅ **Chi-square**, **Wald test**   | ✅ Chi-square checks goodness-of-fit. ❌ t-test assumes normal distribution of residuals, which doesn't hold here. |
| **Decision Tree**             | White    | Tree is easy to interpret; rules are explicit                                                 | ✅ **Chi-square**, **ANOVA**       | ✅ Chi-square tests splits on categories. ❌ t-test assumes continuous distribution. |
| **KNN**                       | Gray     | Prediction is neighbor-based; logic is visible but not formally structured                   | ✅ **Cross-validation**, **Distance-based metrics** | ✅ No model assumptions; KNN is non-parametric. ❌ ANOVA/t-test assume distribution, which KNN doesn’t use. |
| **SVM (nonlinear)**           | Black    | Kernel trick hides decision surface logic                                                    | ✅ **t-test**, **ROC-AUC**, **Permutation** | ✅ Permutation is robust. ❌ Chi-square assumes categorical structure; SVM handles complex real-valued features. |
| **Naive Bayes**               | White    | Probabilistic and interpretable; assumes independence                                        | ✅ **Chi-square**, **Likelihood Ratio** | ✅ Handles categorical features. ❌ t-test not ideal due to non-normal and discrete nature of variables. |
| **Random Forest**             | Black    | Many trees → hard to explain full decision logic                                             | ✅ **Permutation test**, **Chi-square** | ✅ Robust to non-parametric structure. ❌ t-test unsuitable due to ensemble complexity. |
| **Gradient Boosting (XGBoost)** | Black  | Sequential learners; interpretable via SHAP, but overall model is opaque                    | ✅ **ANOVA**, **Cross-validation** | ✅ ANOVA for comparing multiple models. ❌ p-test not applicable without linear form. |
| **AdaBoost**                  | Black    | Boosted weak learners; model logic is scattered                                              | ✅ **McNemar’s**, **Permutation**  | ✅ Best for paired classifiers. ❌ ANOVA fails to account for dependency in boosting. |
| **K-Means**                   | Gray     | Centroids interpretable, clusters aren't labeled                                             | ✅ **ANOVA**, **Silhouette Score** | ✅ ANOVA compares clusters. ❌ Chi-square assumes labeled categories which clustering lacks. |
| **Hierarchical Clustering**   | Gray     | Dendrogram is partially interpretable                                                        | ✅ **ANOVA**, **Cophenetic Correlation** | ✅ Validates dendrogram consistency. ❌ t-test assumes grouped variables beforehand. |
| **PCA**                       | White    | Projection coefficients are transparent                                                      | ✅ **ANOVA**, **Eigenvalue analysis** | ✅ PCA assumes variance capture; ANOVA tests significance. ❌ Chi-square unsuitable for continuous components. |
| **Neural Networks (MLP)**     | Black    | Layers and weights are hidden; outputs are not easily traced                                 | ✅ **Permutation**, **t-test**     | ✅ Non-parametric test suits black box nature. ❌ Chi-square can't handle continuous outputs. |
| **CNN**                       | Black    | Visual patterns captured by filters; layers not interpretable                                | ✅ **Grad-CAM**, **AUC**, **Ablation** | ✅ Visual tools show relevance. ❌ Traditional statistical tests fail due to complex architecture. |
| **RNN / LSTM / GRU**          | Black    | Time dependencies and states are hard to interpret                                           | ✅ **Time-series cross-validation**, **t-test** | ✅ Time-aware evaluation needed. ❌ ANOVA assumes independence. |
| **Transformers (BERT, GPT)**  | Black    | Attention is partially interpretable; overall extremely complex                              | ✅ **Attention visualization**, **BLEU**, **AUC** | ✅ NLP-specific metrics like BLEU better for evaluation. ❌ t-test irrelevant in text generation. |
| **Autoencoders**              | Black    | Latent space is abstract; reconstruction errors used instead of predictions                  | ✅ **Reconstruction Error**, **ANOVA** | ✅ Compare across architectures. ❌ Chi-square doesn’t apply due to lack of labels. |
| **GANs**                      | Black    | Generative logic split across generator and discriminator                                    | ✅ **FID Score**, **Inception Score** | ✅ Image quality metrics more suited. ❌ Classical tests can't evaluate image realism. |
| **Deep Belief Networks**      | Black    | Layers of hidden units; complex pretraining                                                   | ✅ **ANOVA**, **Reconstruction Loss** | ✅ Model evaluation via loss. ❌ Chi-square irrelevant without labeled output. |

---

### 🧠 Summary: When to Use What Test

| Test            | Best For Evaluating...                                                                            | Avoid When...                                                                 |
|-----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **t-test**       | Mean accuracy/difference between two models (parametric)                                         | Model is non-parametric or output isn’t normally distributed                   |
| **p-value**      | Significance of features/coefficients in linear models                                           | Model is non-linear or uninterpretable                                        |
| **Chi-square**   | Categorical predictions/outputs (e.g., label distributions)                                      | Outputs or features are continuous                                             |
| **ANOVA**        | Comparing **3+ models or groups** on performance metrics                                         | Data doesn’t meet normality/variance assumptions                               |
| **Permutation**  | Black-box models, no parametric assumptions                                                      | Large datasets where permutations are too costly                               |
| **McNemar’s**    | Paired classification results (e.g., same samples, different models)                            | Models are not evaluated on **exact same samples**                             |

